[722,0,0,1912,"Mother and Child","A baby sleeps in a mother's arms in this early color photograph by the British amateur Henry Essenhigh Corke. The warm reds and browns come from the tiny dyed grains in an Autochrome plate.","What would it feel like to hold a sleeping baby?"],
[723,1,0,1912,"A Log Cabin in the Forest","Prokudin-Gorsky photographed a small log hut in a green clearing in Russia in color. The grass and trees look as bright and fresh as they would in a modern snapshot.","Who might live in a hut like this?"],
[727,4,0,1912,"The Titanic Leaving Southampton","The Titanic, the largest ship in the world, left Southampton, England, on April 10, 1912, on her first voyage. She sank four days later after hitting an iceberg, and about 1,500 people lost their lives.","How do you think the people on shore felt as she left?"],
[736,0,0,1913,"Ezra Pound","Ezra Pound was an American poet who lived in London and helped many other writers get their start. Alvin Langdon Coburn photographed him with a dramatic, intense look.","What kind of poetry do you think he wrote?"],
[737,3,0,1913,"Eight-Year-Old Jack Milking the Cow","A boy crouches beside a cow, milking it by hand. Lewis Hine photographed farm children too, since many worked from early morning with their families, long before school.","What chores do you have each day?"],
[740,5,0,1914,"Nandi Bull, Benares","A bright orange bull statue with flower garlands stands near a Hindu temple in Varanasi, India. Banker Albert Kahn sent photographers around the world with color Autochrome plates for his Archives of the Planet, and Stéphane Passet took this one.","How old do you think the statue is?"],
[741,5,0,1921,"The Taj Mahal","A path lined with tall cypress trees leads toward the Taj Mahal in India. This early color picture shows the white marble tomb glowing pink in the light.","Where does the path seem to lead?"],
[742,2,0,1914,"Boulevard de Bonne-Nouvelle, Paris","Stéphane Passet photographed Paris in color just before the First World War. The tall buildings and empty street look blue in the early light.","Why do you think the street is empty?"],
[743,2,0,1914,"À la Grappe d'Or, Paris","A shop in Paris with bright red and gold paint, photographed in color in 1914. Early Autochromes captured the colors of ordinary streets before they changed.","What would you buy at a shop like this?"],
[746,1,0,1915,"From the Back-Window, \"291\"","From the back window of his New York gallery, Alfred Stieglitz photographed a snow-covered tree and the buildings beyond. He made many pictures of the same view in different seasons.","Choose a window at home. What would it look like in different seasons?"],
[757,1,0,1915,"St. Cloud","Eugène Atget photographed the park at Saint-Cloud, outside Paris, at dawn when it was empty. Two great trees stand over a still pool that doubles them.","What do you notice about the water?"],
[759,1,0,1915,"The Endurance at Sunset","Frank Hurley was the photographer on Ernest Shackleton's Antarctic expedition. The ship Endurance sits frozen in sea ice under a pink sky, in an image Hurley colored by hand.","How would you feel stuck in the ice?"],
[761,2,0,1915,"Wall Street","Paul Strand photographed small figures hurrying past the huge dark windows of a bank on Wall Street in New York. He made the people look tiny and the building look like a wall, and it became a landmark of modern photography.","How do the huge windows make the people feel?"],
[762,4,0,1915,"The Endurance at Night","Hurley lit the ship Endurance with flashbulbs at night, with the rigging coated in frost. Soon after, the ice crushed the ship, but all twenty-eight men survived by dragging lifeboats across the ice.","What would you pack if you had to leave a ship in the ice?"],
[763,2,0,1915,"Main Market Square, Kraków","Flower sellers and shoppers fill the great square of Kraków in this Autochrome by Tadeusz Rząca. Autochromes made on glass look like stained glass, glowing with color.","What do you think the people are buying?"],
[764,2,0,1915,"The Panama-Pacific Exposition","San Francisco held a huge world's fair in 1915 to celebrate the opening of the Panama Canal. Arnold Genthe photographed its colonnades and domes in color.","What would you want to see at a world's fair?"],
[766,8,0,1915,"Autochrome Still Life","Vegetables, oranges and a jug sit on a table, glowing like stained glass. Autochrome was made on glass plates, and the colors have lasted more than a century.","Which food do you want to eat?"],
